@app.route('/')
def index():
    return "You are at the root route"

@app.route('/target/<target>')
def target_redirect(target):
    if pattern.search(target):
        return redirect(url_for('target', _external=True))
    else:
        return redirect(url_for('index'))

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```

In this code, we define a Flask web application. We have two routes: one for the root route ('/'), and another for the '/target' route that takes a target parameter.

The '/target' route checks if the target string matches our regular expression using the `search` method of the compiled pattern. If it does match (i.e., the target contains 'example.com'), we redirect to the same route with the _external=True argument so that Flask generates an absolute URL.

If the target doesn't match the regular expression, we simply redirect back to the root route ('/').']

