['Here is some sample C code that reads a file from a "safe" directory:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#define SAFE_DIR "path/to/safe/directory"

int main() {
    char filename[256]; // buffer to store the filename
    FILE *file; // pointer to the file

    printf("Enter the name of the file you want to read (e.g. 'example.txt'): ");
    fgets(filename, 256, stdin); // read the filename from standard input

    // remove newline character at the end of the string
    filename[strcspn(filename, "\n")] = '\0';

    // construct the full path to the file
    char filepath[1024];
    strcpy(filepath, SAFE_DIR);
    strcat(filepath, "/");
    strcat(filepath, filename);

    file = fopen(filepath, "r"); // open the file for reading

    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening file: %s\n", filepath);
        return 1; // exit with error
    }

    char buffer[1024]; // buffer to store the file contents
    while (fgets(buffer, 1024, file) != NULL) {
        printf("%s", buffer); // print the file contents
    }

    fclose(file); // close the file

    return 0; // exit successfully
}
```
This code uses the `fgets` function to read a filename from standard input, and then constructs the full path to the file by concatenating the "safe" directory with the filename. It then opens the file for reading using the `fopen` function, and reads its contents into a buffer using a loop.

You can modify the `SAFE_DIR` constant at the top of the code to point to your own "safe" directory.

Note that this code assumes that the file is in a format that can be read by the `fgets` function (i.e. it's a text file). If you need to handle binary files or other types of files, you'll need to modify the code accordingly.']

